  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/c4e03ef210691e4eee922d0c35e90abb9cde1a78064ad491d4ac084d875c4a57.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/40b5e9b8928be63a415877c0633f0302c874db51b1c74a1f82b7b1526a6f4d14.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-1$ and goes down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/c68544957f33afdeab436a3fbf75b9e27990a2e7f3ea75f39c6e58dce24f6185.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/4c91ba8f06642fe14035400efa61dbd2d8bbadd07274fedc9a90678970bbb9a8.png)\n\n \n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
